    answer: 1,
    explanation: "Data masking transforms sensitive data into realistic-looking but fictitious data that maintains format, referential integrity, and statistical distributions. This makes it ideal for development and testing environments where realistic data is needed but actual PII must not be exposed. Encryption produces unreadable ciphertext (not useful for testing). Tokenization creates tokens that don't resemble the original data format. Physical destruction obviously eliminates the data.",
