    explanation: "Entrapment occurs when law enforcement induces a person to commit a crime that the person was not otherwise predisposed to commit, and it can serve as a defense against the resulting charges. Enticement is the related but lawful practice of making an already-motivated offender more likely to target a monitored resource, such as a honeypot that attracts an intruder who was already attempting intrusions, and the key distinction is that the criminal intent existed beforehand. Due diligence refers to the investigation and ongoing verification an organization performs to understand its risks and obligations, which has no application to law enforcement conduct during an operation. Exigent circumstances describe an emergency condition permitting a search or seizure without a warrant, which is a separate doctrine concerning evidence collection rather than inducement to commit an offense.",
