    explanation: "Maximum Tolerable Downtime is the absolute limit of disruption a business function can survive, so the Recovery Time Objective, which is the planned restoration target, must fall at or below that ceiling; if the RTO exceeded the MTD the recovery plan would by definition allow unacceptable damage. Stating the RTO must be greater than the MTD inverts the relationship and would guarantee the organization misses its own survival threshold. Requiring the two values to be identical is wrong because a prudent plan usually sets the RTO below the MTD to leave room for work recovery time and unforeseen delays. The claim that RTO covers data and MTD covers systems misstates both terms, since the data loss tolerance metric is the Recovery Point Objective and both RTO and MTD describe time-based tolerance for the disruption of a business function.",
