    explanation: "Addresses in the fe80::/10 range are IPv6 link-local addresses, which every IPv6 interface configures automatically and which are valid only on the local link — they are never forwarded by routers, so they appear even when no router advertisement has been received. A global unicast address is globally routable and typically begins with 2000::/3; obtaining one normally requires either DHCPv6 or a router advertisement carrying a prefix. A unique local address is the IPv6 analogue of RFC 1918 private space and uses the fc00::/7 range (in practice fd00::/8), not fe80::. A multicast address in IPv6 always begins with ff00::/8, and multicast addresses identify groups of receivers rather than a single interface.",
