    answer: 0,
    explanation: "Implicit deny, also called default deny, means that any request not explicitly permitted by a policy is refused. It is a foundational secure-design behavior in firewalls, authorization engines, and access control lists alike, ensuring that gaps in policy result in no access rather than unintended access. Fail-open processing is the opposite behavior, granting access when a control cannot reach a decision or a component fails; it favors availability over confidentiality and would have allowed the request. Transitive trust describes a relationship in which trusting one entity extends trust to entities that entity trusts, which is a concern in federation and domain trust design but is not the reason an unmatched request is refused. Discretionary override implies that some party can bypass the policy decision, which is not a recognized access control principle and is contrary to what happened, since no exception was applied.",
