    explanation: "A credentialed (authenticated) scan supplies the scanner with valid account credentials so it can log in to the target and inspect installed software versions, patch levels, and configuration settings directly, producing far more accurate results with fewer false positives than an unauthenticated scan. A passive scan observes network traffic without sending probes to targets at all and therefore cannot log in to anything. A port scan only enumerates which network ports are open and which services appear to be listening; it does not authenticate or examine internal system state. An external perimeter scan describes the scanner's vantage point (outside the network boundary) rather than whether it authenticates, and such scans are typically unauthenticated views of internet-facing services.",
