    answer: 3,
    explanation: "A software escrow agreement is correct because it places a copy of the source code and build materials with an independent third party, to be released to the customer if defined conditions occur, most commonly the vendor's bankruptcy or its failure to continue supporting the product. That is exactly the continuity risk described. A software bill of materials inventories the components inside a product, which supports vulnerability response and license management but confers no rights to the source code and would not let the hospital maintain the product itself. A service level agreement defines performance and support commitments while the vendor is still operating and provides no remedy once the vendor no longer exists. A code signing certificate lets the vendor digitally sign its releases so customers can verify publisher identity and file integrity; it addresses tampering, not the vendor's survival.",
