    explanation: "Memory-hard functions such as Argon2id are designed on the observation that the advantage of GPUs and application-specific hardware comes from replicating a small arithmetic circuit thousands of times. By requiring each hash computation to allocate and repeatedly access a large region of memory, the function makes memory bandwidth and capacity the limiting factor, so an attacker can run far fewer guesses in parallel per device, and the cost advantage of custom hardware over an ordinary server shrinks dramatically. Digest length is a separate parameter and collision resistance is not the property that matters when an attacker is testing guesses against a known digest. A per-user salt is still required and is supplied independently; the memory cost does not make hashes unique across users, and without a salt identical passwords would still produce identical digests. The memory parameter does not encrypt anything, though a separately stored secret value, sometimes called a pepper, is a distinct additional control that can be layered on top.",
