    answer: 0,
    explanation: "Hostname verification in current clients is performed against the subject alternative name extension; the common name field is deprecated for this purpose and major browsers no longer fall back to it, so a certificate that names the host only in the common name is rejected as a name mismatch regardless of how exactly the string matches. The CA must issue the certificate with a subject alternative name entry for every DNS name the service uses. Key length affects whether the certificate meets cryptographic policy minimums and would produce a weak-key or algorithm error, not a name mismatch. The extended key usage extension declares the purposes for which the certificate may be used and its absence or misconfiguration causes a usage error rather than a hostname error. Adding a wildcard to the common name does not help, because the client is not consulting the common name at all; a wildcard would have to appear in the subject alternative name to have any effect.",
