    answer: 1,
    explanation: "Double encryption falls to a meet-in-the-middle attack. Given a known plaintext and ciphertext pair, the attacker encrypts the plaintext under all possible values of the first key and stores the intermediate results, then decrypts the ciphertext under all possible values of the second key and looks for a match. The work is on the order of two single-key searches rather than the product of them, so the effective strength is only marginally better than a single 56-bit key, at the cost of a very large storage requirement. Triple application with the encrypt-decrypt-encrypt structure raises the attack cost enough to be useful while retaining backward compatibility when all three keys are equal. Double encryption is not algebraically collapsible to a single-key encryption for DES, which is not a group, so that claim is false, though for some ciphers such closure would indeed be fatal. Encrypting twice does not change the ciphertext length, since each pass maps a 64-bit block to a 64-bit block. Weak keys exist in DES and should be avoided, but they are a small enumerated set and are not created or reintroduced by the second encryption pass.",
