    answer: 2,
    explanation: "SSH provides server authentication by having the client compare the host key presented during the key exchange against the fingerprint it recorded on first connection. A changed fingerprint means the traffic is terminating on a different key than before, which is the exact signature of an interception device inserted between client and server, though it can also result from a legitimate rebuild or key regeneration. Because the administrator confirms no maintenance occurred, the correct response is to treat it as a potential man-in-the-middle attack and verify the true fingerprint through a separate channel such as console access or the configuration management system before trusting the session. Clock drift is incorrect because SSH host key verification compares a hash of the public key and does not depend on time validity the way certificate checking does. An expired client private key is incorrect because SSH user keys have no built-in expiration and, more importantly, a user key problem would produce an authentication failure rather than a host identification warning. Negotiating a weaker cipher is incorrect because cipher selection has no effect on the stored host key fingerprint, and suppressing the warning by changing algorithms would ignore the actual risk.",
