    answer: 0,
    explanation: "A transitive or indirect dependency is one that a project acquires because a package it does declare depends on it, and such components are compiled or packaged into the deliverable and are just as exploitable as direct dependencies; the durable fix is to move the declared parent to a version that resolves to the patched library, or to declare an explicit version override or resolution when no such parent release exists. Dependency confusion is an attack in which a public package with the same name as an internal one is resolved in preference to the internal package, which is not what the report describes. Calling it a false positive is wrong because dependency resolution routinely places packages in the final artifact that never appear in the top-level manifest, and that artifact is what runs in production. Typosquatting relies on a developer misspelling a package name when declaring it, whereas here the package was never declared at all and arrived through a legitimate parent.",
