    answer: 0,
    explanation: "In a Platform as a Service model the provider manages and patches everything up to and including the operating system, middleware, and language runtime, while the customer retains responsibility for the application code it deploys, that application's configuration, and its data, so the runtime vulnerability belongs to the provider and the authorization flaw belongs to the customer. Reversing the assignment is wrong because the customer has no ability to patch a managed runtime it does not control and the provider has no visibility into or right to alter customer application logic. Assigning both to the provider is the common misconception that moving to a managed platform transfers application security, which it never does; the provider cannot know the customer's intended authorization rules. Assigning both to the customer contradicts the defining characteristic of the service model, since the customer neither controls nor can update the managed platform layers.",
